# compound-controls-spec.md

Audited against HEAD `12a4395`. Every file path, column name and function name
below is real. Where this spec contradicts an existing code comment, the
contradiction is called out explicitly — do not resolve it silently.

---

## Goal

Five changes to how a user controls a compound after it exists.

1. **Pause** a compound or a whole stack for a stretch of days, with a resume
   date or indefinitely, backdateable.
2. **Rebuild the compound detail sheet** so every action is visible without a
   `More` disclosure, and the fill shown is the real one.
3. **Bulk powder as a fourth inventory form**, plus real container fill for tubs
   and bottles instead of the illustrative level.
4. **Log a compound more than once a day** where `times_per_day > 1`.
5. **One-off logs** — record something taken once, with no schedule and no
   stock.

---

## Out of Scope

**DO NOT add an "End" or "Archive" lifecycle verb.** Delete is already soft
(`archiveProtocolCompound`, `lib/home/protocolSync.ts:479-482`) and keeps every
dose log. Delete stays the only lifecycle verb; Pause is the second and last
addition. The comment at `components/home/CompoundDetailSheet.tsx:300-302`
becomes "Delete and Pause are the ONLY lifecycle verbs" — update it, don't
delete it.

**DO NOT store a "remaining" or "paused" derived value anywhere.** The
no-stored-derived invariant (`supabase/trackd_schema_v0_4_2.sql:762-767`,
`lib/db/inventory.ts:10-13`) holds for pauses too. A pause is an interval; every
consequence of it is computed at read time.

**DO NOT push `cycles.ended_on` or `protocol_compounds.cycle_end_date` when a
pause extends a cycle.** `recordScheduleVersion` deletes every version dated
after the one it writes (`lib/home/stack.ts:275`), so writing an end date from a
past day destroys future schedule versions. Pause extension is subtraction
inside the cycle-day arithmetic, never a write.

**DO NOT put pause state in columns on `protocol_compounds`.** A compound is
paused many times over its life. Columns hold one pause, and overwriting an old
pause retroactively converts its days back into missed days, because missed is
inferred from due-and-unlogged (`components/home/HomeScreen.tsx:356`).

**DO NOT set `is_active = false` on a paused compound.** Pause and archive are
different states. `is_active` is what Delete writes; a paused compound is still
live, still in the stack, still holding stock.

**DO NOT build a job that resumes a pause.** A pause with an end date resumes
because the date comparison stops matching, not because anything ran. No cron,
no scheduled function, no "resume" write. The only writes are creating a pause
and ending one early.

**DO NOT reuse `dose_logs` for one-off logs.** `protocol_compound_id` is NOT
NULL, and `doseLogRowId` is one row per compound per day at both the database
and the device store (`lib/home/doseLog.ts:21`).

**DO NOT add `scoop` to the `dose_unit` enum.** That enum is guarded by three
unit-family triggers protecting inventory maths. One-off log units are a
separate `text` column with its own CHECK.

**DO NOT open the stock form on `reconstituted`.** `TYPES` in
`AddStockSheet.tsx` is ordered with reconstituted first and the picker defaults
to it, so adding stock for creatine or a vitamin starts by asking about BAC
water. The form is resolved from the compound before the sheet renders.

**DO NOT show a form a compound cannot be.** `catalogueForms` already returns
only the real options and the picker already disappears when there is one
(`AddStockSheet.tsx`). The mechanism is fine; what's wrong is the data behind it
— supplements resolve by route, so creatine's `po` produces `oral_solid`.

**DO NOT support different amounts per slot on a multi-dose day in this
version.** A compound dosed 2× daily delivers `dose_amount` twice. Per-slot
amounts (100 mg AM, 50 mg PM) are a later change and need a per-slot column on
the schedule, not just a slot index on the log.

**DO NOT add async reads inside `isDueOnFor`.** It is pure and synchronous and
called by the week strip, calendar grid, consistency and Next Dose
(`lib/home/stack.ts:205-219`). This constraint already shelved the
`when_vial_empty` end condition (`lib/protocol/cycleRule.ts:375-394`).

**DO NOT block a dose log because stock has run out.** Today the write path
never reads remaining and the view clamps at zero. Keep it that way.

