## Design Decisions

**An inventory form is a fact about a compound, not something re-derived from
its name.** `StackCompound` currently carries no inventory type and rebuilds it
from name + route on every render, with the gap already logged
(`lib/containers/form.ts:126-144`). Bulk powder cannot be derived from route —
creatine is `po`, which resolves to `oral_solid`. The form becomes a stored,
nullable column on `protocol_compounds` that falls back to today's derivation
when null. This is the prerequisite for changes 3 and 4 both.

**A pause is a bounded interval in its own table.** Modelled on `blocks`
(`supabase/blocks/001_blocks.sql`), which is the only existing shape in the
schema with a start, a nullable end and a partial unique index. Not a
`stopped` schedule version — a stop is open-ended until superseded and cannot
be written in advance.

**A pause is invisible to adherence because nothing was due.** The gate goes
inside `isDueOnFor`, which makes a paused day return `none` rather than
`missed`. This is why backdating a pause costs nothing: no backfill, no
migration, the reclassification happens at next render. The codebase has been
bitten three times by the opposite (`lib/home/stack.ts:553-566`, `:282-291`).

**Paused days do not advance the cycle clock.** A twelve-week run paused for ten
days is still twelve weeks of dosing. Implemented by subtracting paused days
from the day offset before the on/off modulo in `isOnCycle`, and by adding
remaining pause days to `days_to_empty` in the view. Both are arithmetic, not
writes.

**A stack pause is N compound pauses sharing a group id.** `stack_members` holds
a pairing and an integer and nothing else, deliberately
(`supabase/protocol/007_stacks.sql:9-17`). A stack has nowhere to store a pause
and should not gain one. The group id is what lets Resume restore only what that
action paused, leaving individually-paused members paused.

**Pause is expressed as a duration, not a date.** "Two weeks" is how a user
thinks about a holiday. Until-date and indefinite exist as second choices.
Backdate is a row that opens a picker, not a visible date field.

**The compound detail sheet shows every action at one level.** No `More`
disclosure. Actions group under Today / Protocol / Inventory with Delete
separated below. No Close button — the sheet dismisses by drag.

**The fill on the detail sheet is the real fill.** Line 163 currently hardcodes
`fill={0.7}`, which is a decorative number sitting next to real ones.

**Container fill display stays continuous; the quarter presets stay an input.**
`vialLiquid` is already continuous (`lib/containers/geometry.ts:47-54`) and
`remainingBase / totalBase` already produces the value for any type. The
quarters in `FILL_PRESETS` are the part-used estimator that produces
`prior_used_base` — reuse them for tubs and bottles, don't turn the display into
steps.

**A bulk powder is tracked in grams and nothing else.** `total_amount` is the
tub weight, `base_unit` is `g`. Serving size is an optional convenience that
powers a quick-add chip and a scoop-to-grams conversion; it is never the unit of
inventory.

**Grams get their own unit family.** `unit_family_compatible`
(`supabase/trackd_schema_v0_4_2.sql:604-611`) gains `g` accepting `g` or `mg`.
`g` is deliberately not added to the existing `mg` family, or a user could log
grams against a peptide vial.

**Pause dates are inclusive at both ends.** `started_on` is the first paused day
and `ends_on` is the last. Resuming today writes `ends_on` as yesterday, so today
is due again. Undefined inclusivity is the single most likely source of an
off-by-one here, and an off-by-one shows up as a phantom missed day.

**A pause shifts the cycle, not the cadence grid.** Paused days are subtracted
from the on/off cycle arithmetic, because losing on-days would cut a protocol
short. They are **not** subtracted inside `isDueOn`, so an every-third-day
compound resumes on its original grid rather than re-anchoring to the resume
date. Re-anchoring would mean every pause silently rewrites the calendar for
every future dose, which is worse than the next dose landing one or two days
off.

**An end date is extended at read time, never written.** For a compound with
`cycle_end_type = 'on_date'`, the effective end is the stored date plus the
paused days that fall before it. Without this, pausing near the end of a run just
eats the tail. The stored `cycle_end_date` never moves — see Out of Scope.

**Pausing something already paused edits that pause.** The action is not additive.
If a pause covering today exists, the sheet opens showing that pause with Resume
and Change end date, not a fresh pause form. A new pause whose range overlaps an
existing one extends the existing row rather than inserting a second.

**A one-off log is the one write in the app with a random id.** Everything else
uses a deterministic id so replay is idempotent (`doseLogRowId`). Two one-off logs
of the same thing on the same day must both survive, so no deterministic key
exists. The id is a client-generated uuid, stored locally at write time and
reused on every replay — which is what preserves idempotency.

**Adding stock asks about the thing you're actually holding.** The stock form is
resolved from the compound's inventory form (Step 1) before the sheet opens. A
peptide asks about powder and BAC water. An anabolic asks about mg/mL. A vitamin
asks about tablet count and strength. A protein or creatine tub asks about
weight. The picker only appears when a compound genuinely has more than one
possible form, and even then it opens on that compound's own form, never on
reconstituted.

**An oral holds its strength in whatever unit is printed on the label.** Today
`oral_solid` forces `base_unit = 'mg'`, so a 5000 IU vitamin D tablet cannot be
represented at all. Orals accept `mg` or `iu`, and the column stops being named
for milligrams.

**An oral's strength is optional, and without it the dose is a count.** There
are two honest ways to hold a tablet. Strength stated — vitamin C at 1000 mg a
tab — so you log 2000 mg and two tablets leave stock. Strength not stated — a
multivitamin, fish oil — so you log 1 tab and one tablet leaves stock. The
second is currently impossible because inventory-backed doses are restricted to
mg/mcg/iu. Both must work.

**The powder stock form is the reconstituted form with the second input
removed.** Same sheet, same `FIELD` and `LABEL` classes, same fill picker, same
`clean`/`num` input handling. One amount row instead of two, no derived
concentration readout, and every `hint` subtext dropped.

**A dose slot is identified by index, and existing rows are slot zero.**
`doseLogRowId` gains a fourth argument. A default of 0 makes every existing row
correct without a backfill.

**A slot resolves against the schedule version in force on that day, not the
current one.** Slot 1 means "the second entry in `dose_times`", and `dose_times`
is versioned per `effective_from` (`supabase/protocol/005`). If a user reorders
their times or drops from 3× to 2× daily, resolving old logs against today's
array retroactively relabels history — a dose logged at 20:00 starts displaying
as 14:00. Slots beyond the current `times_per_day` still render; they are past
doses that were genuinely taken, not errors.

**A one-off log references nothing.** No compound, no inventory item, no stack.
It appears in the timeline and nowhere else — excluded from consistency, from
the runway, from stock, from the picker. That exclusion is the entire reason it
can be frictionless.

