### Step 8 — One-off logs

1. Migration `020_one_off_logs.sql`:
   ```
   CREATE TABLE one_off_logs (
       id          uuid PRIMARY KEY,
       user_id     uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES profiles(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
       label       text NOT NULL CHECK (length(btrim(label)) BETWEEN 1 AND 80),
       amount      numeric(10,3),
       unit        text CHECK (unit IN ('g','mg','mcg','iu','ml','tab','capsule','scoop')),
       logged_for  date NOT NULL,
       taken_at    timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
       note        text,
       created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
   );
   ```
   Plus RLS, a `GRANT`, and an index on `(user_id, logged_for)`. `unit` is
   `text` with a CHECK, not `dose_unit` — see Design Decisions.
2. `logged_for` follows the `dose_logs.logged_for` pattern: device-local, only
   written when the device vouches for the day
   (`dayIsDeviceRecorded`, `lib/home/protocolSync.ts:774-785`). Never
   `current_date` in SQL.
3. Union one-off logs into the timeline at read time. Exclude them from
   `DayStatus`, from consistency, from `v_inventory_math`, from the compound
   picker and from Stock.
4. Sheet: recents as chips (distinct label over the last 30 days), then label,
   amount, unit select including `scoop`, then time. Entry point is a row on the
   dashboard below the compound list.
5. After a third log of the same label, offer once to add it to the schedule.
   Dismissal is remembered per label.
6. **Deleting a one-off log.** It will be mis-typed constantly, so it needs a
   delete with a tombstone on the same 14-day TTL pattern, and the delete goes
   through `trackCriticalSync` so hydration can't resurrect it.
7. The id is a client-generated uuid held in localStorage from the moment of
   writing, and the same id is used on every replay. Do not derive it from
   label, date or user — see Design Decisions.

